Every Friday after sunset, the parking lot behind the Ashford library becomes a market. By {{1|eight o'clock in the evening at night}}, more than forty vendors have set up folding tables, and the smell of grilled corn drifts across the street. The market began in 2016 with six stalls, {{2|most of them}} sold vegetables from nearby farms.

{{3|Not only did the market grow quickly, but it also changed}} the neighborhood around it. Storefronts on Elm Street, which had stood empty for years, {{4|now houses}} a bakery, a bike shop, and a tailor. Some longtime residents complain that the crowds make parking {{5|harder then}} it used to be. {{6|Others argue that the market has raised rents, pushing out some of the very families it was meant to serve.}}

The market's founder, a retired teacher named Rosa Ibarra, takes these concerns seriously. Last year she persuaded the city to reserve a third of the stalls for vendors who live within a mile of the market {{7|and waiving their fees}}. {{8|Walking through the market on a Friday night, the mix of languages is striking.}} Vendors call out prices in Spanish, Vietnamese, and Amharic, and {{9|whoever}} buys a mango from the stall near the entrance is likely to be handed a free sample of something else. {{10|Ibarra still works the information table herself most Fridays.}}
